+### §check-fails-closed — a failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22 (replaces §check-fails-open, 2026-09-11)
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files are
+  still only a warning and end in `exit 0`.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e` (`set +e`, `set +o errexit`), and swallowing a command's failure so the check still
+  exits 0.
+- Rejected: fail-open (`set -uo pipefail` without `-e`, always `exit 0`; §check-fails-open). It was adopted after a
+  `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit for a day (2026-09-10).
+  The maintainer overrode it on 2026-10-22. The `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard now skips that case.
+- Reason: maintainer decision (2026-10-22). An internal failure should fail the check, not be skipped silently.
+- Note: under `-e`, a condition that is the last command in a loop body must be written as `if …; then …; fi`, not
+  `[ … ] && …`. A false test there makes the loop, and so the check, exit 1.
